zerkle@iris.ucdavis.edu (Dan Zerkle) writes:

> Here's something fun: Best game I've ever had (isn't it always?), with
> adventures down to level 41, via level teleport traps. However, I have
> yet to find a single scroll of teleport. That's right, folks. I've got
> luckstones, artifacts out my ass (ouch!), every potion and other
> scroll, wands galore, every tool, lots o' genocides, yellow dragon
> scale mail, but no scroll of teleport. I can't get down to the
> interesting levels of Hell, now that I've used up the level teleport
> traps. Growf.

> It's one thing not to win because the Mazes of Menace are dangerous.
> That's ok. It's something else again not to win because the Mazes of
> Menace are boring.

You just need patience.  Since one of every few wandering monsters
carries some artifact other than the quite frequent armor and weapons,
just kill a few thousand wandering monsters, and you should have a
handful of whatever artifact you lack.  I have them in heaps, lately.

"Nethack delivers!"

> Here's a proposal: Put in a little code that will *guarantee* that at
> least one unidentfied scroll and potion will show up on each new
> level. The wands and rings and amulets are less common, and not as
> necessary to the completion of the game, but those teleport scrolls
> are essential. By the time you've seen as many levels as there are
> scrolls, you're guaranteed to have seen all the scrolls.

> It also would be nice to guarantee: at least one altar per alignment,
> appearance of all the special levels (rogue and bigroom, in addition
> to medusa and stronghold), one beehive, one barracks, one zoo, a
> couple of kitchen sinks, one temple, one oracle, and so on. Also, a
> certain amount of random shops would be nice, though not essential.
> The character I mentioned only had two, and was invisible right away,
> so didn't go into either on them on the way down (a pickaxe solved
> that problem).

Yeek! NO! Not every game of nethack should be winable the same way, or
what's the point? I may have played a hundred games before I saw my
first special level, and that's just great, because that's what makes it
special.

Since the things you encounter in each game are a generous or stingy
subset of a large set of possibilities, a nethack guru has to develop
a SET of winning strategies.  If you are guaranteed "one of everything",
then you just develop one boring strategy, and play it every time.  Yuk!
That would completely destroy the real attraction of nethack.

